    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-15yl",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "15YL Alternative arrangements for giving evidence",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-15yl"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-act-1914.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-act-1914.pdf, p.188",
      "values:originalText": "(1) If evidence in a proceeding from a person to whom subsection (3)\napplies is not to be given by means of closed-circuit television (or\nunder Division 2A), the court:\n\n(a) must make arrangements in order to restrict contact\n(including visual contact) that the person may have with any\ndefendant while giving evidence; and\n\n(b) may make arrangements in order to restrict contact (including\nvisual contact) that the person may have with members of the\npublic while giving evidence.\n\n(2) The arrangements may include either of the following:\n\n(a) using screens;\n\n(b) planning seating arrangements for people who have an\ninterest in the proceeding, including:\n\n(i) the level at which they are seated; and\n\n(ii) the people in the person’s line of vision.\nPersons to whom these alternative arrangements apply\n\n(3) This subsection applies to the following persons:\n\n(a) for a child proceeding—a child witness;\n\n(b) for a vulnerable adult proceeding—a vulnerable adult\ncomplainant;\n\n(c) for a special witness proceeding—a special witness for whom\nan order under subsection 15YAB(3) is in force for this\nsection;\nunless that person is at least 16 and chooses not to give evidence\nunder the arrangements.\n15YLA  Recording of evidence given in person\n\n(1) The court must order that evidence given in person by a person to\nwhom subsection (2) applies is to be recorded if:\n\n(a) the court is satisfied that the evidence could be used in\nanother proceeding; and\n\n\nProtecting vulnerable persons  Part IAD\nSpecial facilities for vulnerable persons to give evidence  Division 4\n\nSection 15YLA\n\nCrimes Act 1914\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(b) the court is equipped with the necessary facilities; and\n\n(c) the person agrees to the evidence being recorded.\n\n(2) This subsection applies to the following persons:\n\n(a) for a child proceeding—a child witness;\n\n(b) for a vulnerable adult proceeding—a vulnerable adult\ncomplainant;\n\n(c) for a special witness proceeding—a special witness for whom\nan order under subsection 15YAB(3) is in force for this\nsection.\n\n(3) The recording may be a video or audio recording.\n\n(4) Subsection (1) applies whether or not the evidence is given by\nclosed-circuit television.\n\n\nPart IAD  Protecting vulnerable persons",
      "skos:definition": "(1) If evidence in a proceeding from a person to whom subsection (3)\napplies is not to be given by means of closed-circuit television (or\nunder Division 2A), the court:\n\n(a) must make arrangements in order to restrict contact\n(including visual contact) that the person may have with any\ndefendant while giving evidence; and\n\n(b) may make arrangements in order to restrict contact (including\nvisual contact) that the person may have with members of the\npublic while giving evidence.\n\n(2) The arrangements may include either of the following:\n\n(a) using screens;\n\n(b) planning seating arrangements for people who have an\ninterest in the proceeding, including:\n\n(i) the level at which they are seated; and\n\n(ii) the people in the person’s line of vision.\nPersons to whom these alternative arrangements apply\n\n(3) This subsection applies to the following persons:\n\n(a) for a child proceeding—a child witness;\n\n(b) for a vulnerable adult proceeding—a vulnerable adult\ncomplainant;\n\n(c) for a special witness proceeding—a special witness for whom\nan order under subsection 15YAB(3) is in force for this\nsection;\nunless that person is at least 16 and chooses not to give evidence\nunder the arrangements.\n15YLA  Recording of evidence given in person\n\n(1) The court must order that evidence given in person by a person to\nwhom subsection (2) applies is to be recorded if:\n\n(a) the court is satisfied that the evidence could be used in\nanother proceeding; and\n\n\nProtecting vulnerable persons  Part IAD\nSpecial facilities for vulnerable persons to give evidence  Division 4\n\nSection 15YLA\n\nCrimes Act 1914\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(b) the court is equipped with the necessary facilities; and\n\n(c) the person agrees to the evidence being recorded.\n\n(2) This subsection applies to the following persons:\n\n(a) for a child proceeding—a child witness;\n\n(b) for a vulnerable adult proceeding—a vulnerable adult\ncomplainant;\n\n(c) for a special witness proceeding—a special witness for whom\nan order under subsect",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-15yl-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-15yl-ss-2"
        },
