      "cml:premiseText": "Revocation may occur before the end of the parole period or licence period."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-19au-ss-2",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "19AU(2) Attorney-General may revoke parole order or licence",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-19au-ss-2"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-act-1914.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-act-1914.pdf, p.271",
      "values:originalText": "(2) Before revoking a parole order or a licence, the Attorney-General\nmust, subject to subsection (3), by notice in the prescribed form,\nnotify the person to whom the order or licence relates of:\n\n(a) the condition of the order or licence alleged to have been\nbreached; and\n\n(b) the fact that the Attorney-General proposes to revoke the\norder or licence at the end of 14 days after the day the notice\nis issued unless the person, within that period, gives the\nAttorney-General written reasons why the order or licence\nshould not be revoked and those reasons are accepted by the\nAttorney-General.",
      "skos:definition": "(2) Before revoking a parole order or a licence, the Attorney-General\nmust, subject to subsection (3), by notice in the prescribed form,\nnotify the person to whom the order or licence relates of:\n\n(a) the condition of the order or licence alleged to have been\nbreached; and\n\n(b) the fact that the Attorney-General proposes to revoke the\norder or licence at the end of 14 days after the day the notice\nis issued unless the person, within that period, gives the\nAttorney-General written reasons why the order or licence\nshould not be revoked and those reasons are accepted by the\nAttorney-General.",
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-19au"
      },
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-19au-ss-2-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-19au-ss-2-logic-deontic-1"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "d61ffde98299a634c81c76b513f5ea1969ac68c6cc6ef148408a6fafb70cf8c6",
      "skos:note": "The Attorney-General must notify the person to whom the order or licence relates of certain conditions before revoking it.",
      "dc:description": "Notice in the prescribed form"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-19au-ss-2",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "19AU(2) Attorney-General may revoke parole order or licence",
      "values:kind": "subsection",
      "cof:pageNumber": 271,
      "values:originalText": "(2) Before revoking a parole order or a licence, the Attorney-General\nmust, subject to subsection (3), by notice in the prescribed form,\nnotify the person to whom the order or licence relates of:\n\n(a) the condition of the order or licence alleged to have been\nbreached; and\n\n(b) the fact that the Attorney-General proposes to revoke the\norder or licence at the end of 14 days after the day the notice\nis issued unless the person, within that period, gives the\nAttorney-General written reasons why the order or licence\nshould not be revoked and those reasons are accepted by the\nAttorney-General."
    },
