    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-20bh",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "20BH State or Territory mental health authorities to be notified of",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-20bh"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-act-1914.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-act-1914.pdf, p.310",
      "values:originalText": "certain releases\n\nWhere a person detained by authority of an order under\nsubsection 20BC(2) or a warrant under subsection 20BF(5) for a\nspecified period in a State or Territory is due to be released\nbecause the period of that person’s detention has ended, the\nAttorney-General must notify the mental health authorities of the\nState or Territory of the proposed release of the person.",
      "skos:definition": "certain releases\n\nWhere a person detained by authority of an order under\nsubsection 20BC(2) or a warrant under subsection 20BF(5) for a\nspecified period in a State or Territory is due to be released\nbecause the period of that person’s detention has ended, the\nAttorney-General must notify the mental health authorities of the\nState or Territory of the proposed release of the person.",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-20bh-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-20bh-logic-deontic-1"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "1ff53d9d17dc03aec5ea9f487fd605d021776e67db7c443718c22fcc41c1bca4",
      "skos:note": "Notify mental health authorities of proposed release"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-20bh",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "20BH State or Territory mental health authorities to be notified of",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 310,
      "values:originalText": "certain releases\n\nWhere a person detained by authority of an order under\nsubsection 20BC(2) or a warrant under subsection 20BF(5) for a\nspecified period in a State or Territory is due to be released\nbecause the period of that person’s detention has ended, the\nAttorney-General must notify the mental health authorities of the\nState or Territory of the proposed release of the person."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-20bh-norm",
      "@type": "values:Obligation",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-20bh"
      },
      "values:deonticStatus": {
        "@id": "values:HeuristicDerived"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "values:borneBy": {
        "@id": "values:Agent"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-20bh-logic-deontic-1",
      "@type": "cml:LogicApplication",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "cml:operator": "MUST",
      "cml:logicSummary": "Obligation to notify",
      "cml:executionSurface": "modalities::logic::deontic",
      "cml:confidence": {
        "@value": 1.0,
        "@type": "xsd:decimal"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-20bi",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "20BI Appeals against some Victorian jury findings of unfitness to be",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-20bi"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-act-1914.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-act-1914.pdf, p.310",
      "values:originalText": "tried\n\n(1) This section applies if:\n\n(a) in proceedings for a federal offence in the Trial Division of\nthe Supreme Court of Victoria or in the County Court of\nVictoria, a question arises whether the accused is fit to stand\ntrial; and\n\n\nSentencing, imprisonment and release of federal offenders  Part IB\nUnfitness to be tried  Division 6\n\nSection 20BI\n\nCrimes Act 1914\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(b) a jury finds that the accused is unfit.\nRight to appeal\n\n(2) The accused may appeal to the Court of Appeal of Victoria against\nthe finding:\n\n(a) on a ground involving only one or more questions of law\nalone; or\n\n(b) on a ground involving one or more questions of fact alone, or\none or more questions of mixed law and fact, if the judge\nbefore whom the accused came for trial certifies the ground\nis fit for appeal; or\n\n(c) on any ground if the Court of Appeal gives leave.\nDecision on appeal\n\n(3) The Court of Appeal must allow the appeal if the court thinks that:\n\n(a) the finding should be set aside because it is unreasonable or\ncannot be supported having regard to the evidence; or\n\n(b) the order of the Supreme Court or County Court giving effect\nto the finding should be set aside because of a wrong\ndecision on a question of law; or\n\n(c) there was a miscarriage of justice.\n\n(4) Otherwise, the Court of Appeal must dismiss the appeal.\n\n(5) Despite subsection (3), the Court of Appeal may dismiss the appeal\nif the Court of Appeal thinks that no substantial miscarriage of\njustice has occurred.\nConsequences if appeal allowed\n\n(6) If the Court of Appeal allows the appeal:\n\n(a) the accused may be tried for the federal offence; and\n\n(b) the Court of Appeal may make orders for the custody or bail\nof the accused.",
      "skos:definition": "tried\n\n(1) This section applies if:\n\n(a) in proceedings for a federal offence in the Trial Division of\nthe Supreme Court of Victoria or in the County Court of\nVictoria, a question arises whether the accused is fit to stand\ntrial; and\n\n\nSentencing, imprisonment and release of federal offenders  Part IB\nUnfitness to be tried  Division 6\n\nSection 20BI\n\nCrimes Act 1914\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(b) a jury finds that the accused is unfit.\nRight to appeal\n\n(2) The accused may appeal to the Court of Appeal of Victoria against\nthe finding:\n\n(a) on a ground involving only one or more questions of law\nalone; or\n\n(b) on a ground involving one or more questions of fact alone, or\none or more questions of mixed law and fact, if the judge\nbefore whom the accused came for trial certifies the ground\nis fit for appeal; or\n\n(c) on any ground if the Court of Appeal gives leave.\nDecision on appeal\n\n(3) The Court of Appeal must allow the appeal if the court thinks that:\n\n(a) the finding should be set aside because it is unreasonable or\ncannot be supported having regard to the evidence; or\n\n(b) the order of the Supreme Court or County Court giving effect\nto the finding should be set aside because of a wrong\ndecision on a question of law; or\n\n(c) there was a miscarriage of justice.\n\n(4) Otherwise, the Court of Appeal must dismiss the appeal.\n\n(5) Despite subsection (3), the Court of Appeal may dismiss the appeal\nif the Court of Appeal thinks that no substantial miscarriage of\njustice has occurred.\nConsequences if appeal allowed\n\n(6) If the Court of Appeal allows the appeal:\n\n(a) the accused may be tried for the federal offence; and\n\n(b) the Court of Appeal may make orders for the custody or bail\nof the accused.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-20bi-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-20bi-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-20bi-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-20bi-ss-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-20bi-ss-5"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-20bi-ss-6"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "b90bc9390bcd6bad933160c81fb4d7016601531fdbdcfeb8c31f71904bd9673b"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-20bi",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "20BI Appeals against some Victorian jury findings of unfitness to be",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 310,
      "values:originalText": "tried\n\n(1) This section applies if:\n\n(a) in proceedings for a federal offence in the Trial Division of\nthe Supreme Court of Victoria or in the County Court of\nVictoria, a question arises whether the accused is fit to stand\ntrial; and\n\n\nSentencing, imprisonment and release of federal offenders  Part IB\nUnfitness to be tried  Division 6\n\nSection 20BI\n\nCrimes Act 1914\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(b) a jury finds that the accused is unfit.\nRight to appeal\n\n(2) The accused may appeal to the Court of Appeal of Victoria against\nthe finding:\n\n(a) on a ground involving only one or more questions of law\nalone; or\n\n(b) on a ground involving one or more questions of fact alone, or\none or more questions of mixed law and fact, if the judge\nbefore whom the accused came for trial certifies the ground\nis fit for appeal; or\n\n(c) on any ground if the Court of Appeal gives leave.\nDecision on appeal\n\n(3) The Court of Appeal must allow the appeal if the court thinks that:\n\n(a) the finding should be set aside because it is unreasonable or\ncannot be supported having regard to the evidence; or\n\n(b) the order of the Supreme Court or County Court giving effect\nto the finding should be set aside because of a wrong\ndecision on a question of law; or\n\n(c) there was a miscarriage of justice.\n\n(4) Otherwise, the Court of Appeal must dismiss the appeal.\n\n(5) Despite subsection (3), the Court of Appeal may dismiss the appeal\nif the Court of Appeal thinks that no substantial miscarriage of\njustice has occurred.\nConsequences if appeal allowed\n\n(6) If the Court of Appeal allows the appeal:\n\n(a) the accused may be tried for the federal offence; and\n\n(b) the Court of Appeal may make orders for the custody or bail\nof the accused."
    },
