    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-20bj",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "20BJ Acquittal where person mentally ill",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-20bj"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-act-1914.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-act-1914.pdf, p.313",
      "values:originalText": "(1) Where a person has been charged with a federal offence on\nindictment and the person is acquitted because of mental illness at\nthe time of the offence, the court must order that the person be\ndetained in safe custody in prison or in a hospital for a period\nspecified in the order, not exceeding the maximum period of\nimprisonment that could have been imposed if the person had been\nconvicted of the offence charged.\n\n(2) The Attorney-General may, at any time, by order in writing, vary\nthe prison or hospital at which a person is detained under\nsubsection (1).\n\n(3) Where, for urgent medical or security reasons it becomes necessary\nto do so, an officer of the State or Territory in which a person is\ndetained under this section may vary the prison or hospital at\nwhich the person is detained but, where the officer does so, the\nofficer must forthwith notify the Attorney-General, in writing, of\nthe variation and of the reasons for the variation.\n\n(4) Despite subsection (1), the court may, if in the court’s opinion it is\nmore appropriate to do so than to make an order under\nsubsection (1), order the person’s release from custody either\nabsolutely or subject to conditions to apply for such period as the\ncourt specifies in the order, not exceeding 3 years.\n\n(5) The conditions may include:\n\n(a) a condition that the person remain in the care of a responsible\nperson nominated in the order; and\n\n(b) a condition that the person attend upon a person nominated,\nor at a place specified, in the order for assessment of the\nperson’s mental illness, mental condition or intellectual\ndisability and, where appropriate, for treatment.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) Where a person has been charged with a federal offence on\nindictment and the person is acquitted because of mental illness at\nthe time of the offence, the court must order that the person be\ndetained in safe custody in prison or in a hospital for a period\nspecified in the order, not exceeding the maximum period of\nimprisonment that could have been imposed if the person had been\nconvicted of the offence charged.\n\n(2) The Attorney-General may, at any time, by order in writing, vary\nthe prison or hospital at which a person is detained under\nsubsection (1).\n\n(3) Where, for urgent medical or security reasons it becomes necessary\nto do so, an officer of the State or Territory in which a person is\ndetained under this section may vary the prison or hospital at\nwhich the person is detained but, where the officer does so, the\nofficer must forthwith notify the Attorney-General, in writing, of\nthe variation and of the reasons for the variation.\n\n(4) Despite subsection (1), the court may, if in the court’s opinion it is\nmore appropriate to do so than to make an order under\nsubsection (1), order the person’s release from custody either\nabsolutely or subject to conditions to apply for such period as the\ncourt specifies in the order, not exceeding 3 years.\n\n(5) The conditions may include:\n\n(a) a condition that the person remain in the care of a responsible\nperson nominated in the order; and\n\n(b) a condition that the person attend upon a person nominated,\nor at a place specified, in the order for assessment of the\nperson’s mental illness, mental condition or intellectual\ndisability and, where appropriate, for treatment.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-20bj-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-20bj-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-20bj-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-20bj-ss-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-20bj-ss-5"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "a16c799dc29f23b6061fea5f998e39338c883beb80a1d2bfe8e852770b26e42d"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-20bj",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "20BJ Acquittal where person mentally ill",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 313,
      "values:originalText": "(1) Where a person has been charged with a federal offence on\nindictment and the person is acquitted because of mental illness at\nthe time of the offence, the court must order that the person be\ndetained in safe custody in prison or in a hospital for a period\nspecified in the order, not exceeding the maximum period of\nimprisonment that could have been imposed if the person had been\nconvicted of the offence charged.\n\n(2) The Attorney-General may, at any time, by order in writing, vary\nthe prison or hospital at which a person is detained under\nsubsection (1).\n\n(3) Where, for urgent medical or security reasons it becomes necessary\nto do so, an officer of the State or Territory in which a person is\ndetained under this section may vary the prison or hospital at\nwhich the person is detained but, where the officer does so, the\nofficer must forthwith notify the Attorney-General, in writing, of\nthe variation and of the reasons for the variation.\n\n(4) Despite subsection (1), the court may, if in the court’s opinion it is\nmore appropriate to do so than to make an order under\nsubsection (1), order the person’s release from custody either\nabsolutely or subject to conditions to apply for such period as the\ncourt specifies in the order, not exceeding 3 years.\n\n(5) The conditions may include:\n\n(a) a condition that the person remain in the care of a responsible\nperson nominated in the order; and\n\n(b) a condition that the person attend upon a person nominated,\nor at a place specified, in the order for assessment of the\nperson’s mental illness, mental condition or intellectual\ndisability and, where appropriate, for treatment."
    },
