    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-20bv",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "20BV Psychiatric probation orders",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-20bv"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-act-1914.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-act-1914.pdf, p.324",
      "values:originalText": "(1) Where a person is convicted in a State or Territory of a federal\noffence and the court is satisfied that:\n\n\nSentencing, imprisonment and release of federal offenders  Part IB\nSentencing alternatives for persons suffering from mental illness or intellectual\ndisability  Division 9\n\nSection 20BW\n\nCrimes Act 1914\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(a) the person is suffering from a mental illness within the\nmeaning of the civil law of that State or Territory; and\n\n(b) the illness contributed to the commission of the offence by\nthe person; and\n\n(c) appropriate psychiatric treatment for the person is available\nin a hospital or other place in the State or Territory; and\n\n(d) the person consents to the order being made;\nthe court may, without passing sentence on the person, make an\norder (in this section called a psychiatric probation order) that the\nperson reside at, or attend at, a specified hospital or other place for\nthe purpose of receiving that psychiatric treatment.\n\n(2) The court must not make an order unless the person, or the\nperson’s legal guardian, consents to the proposed treatment.\n\n(3) An order is subject to the following additional conditions:\n\n(a) that the person will, during such period, not exceeding 2\nyears, as the court specifies in the order, be subject to the\nsupervision of a probation officer appointed in accordance\nwith the order and obey all reasonable directions of a\nprobation officer so appointed;\n\n(b) that the person will be of good behaviour for such period, not\nexceeding 5 years, as the court specifies in the order.\n\n(4) The court may, on the application of the person, of the probation\nofficer appointed to supervise the person or of the person in charge\nof the hospital or other place where the treatment is being\nundertaken, vary the treatment that the person is to undertake.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) Where a person is convicted in a State or Territory of a federal\noffence and the court is satisfied that:\n\n\nSentencing, imprisonment and release of federal offenders  Part IB\nSentencing alternatives for persons suffering from mental illness or intellectual\ndisability  Division 9\n\nSection 20BW\n\nCrimes Act 1914\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(a) the person is suffering from a mental illness within the\nmeaning of the civil law of that State or Territory; and\n\n(b) the illness contributed to the commission of the offence by\nthe person; and\n\n(c) appropriate psychiatric treatment for the person is available\nin a hospital or other place in the State or Territory; and\n\n(d) the person consents to the order being made;\nthe court may, without passing sentence on the person, make an\norder (in this section called a psychiatric probation order) that the\nperson reside at, or attend at, a specified hospital or other place for\nthe purpose of receiving that psychiatric treatment.\n\n(2) The court must not make an order unless the person, or the\nperson’s legal guardian, consents to the proposed treatment.\n\n(3) An order is subject to the following additional conditions:\n\n(a) that the person will, during such period, not exceeding 2\nyears, as the court specifies in the order, be subject to the\nsupervision of a probation officer appointed in accordance\nwith the order and obey all reasonable directions of a\nprobation officer so appointed;\n\n(b) that the person will be of good behaviour for such period, not\nexceeding 5 years, as the court specifies in the order.\n\n(4) The court may, on the application of the person, of the probation\nofficer appointed to supervise the person or of the person in charge\nof the hospital or other place where the treatment is being\nundertaken, vary the treatment that the person is to undertake.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-20bv-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-20bv-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-20bv-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-20bv-ss-4"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "e772e430934b4f513e6d08682b90cdf61453e25325d9315891fa7fe9b51c5f11"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-20bv",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "20BV Psychiatric probation orders",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 324,
      "values:originalText": "(1) Where a person is convicted in a State or Territory of a federal\noffence and the court is satisfied that:\n\n\nSentencing, imprisonment and release of federal offenders  Part IB\nSentencing alternatives for persons suffering from mental illness or intellectual\ndisability  Division 9\n\nSection 20BW\n\nCrimes Act 1914\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(a) the person is suffering from a mental illness within the\nmeaning of the civil law of that State or Territory; and\n\n(b) the illness contributed to the commission of the offence by\nthe person; and\n\n(c) appropriate psychiatric treatment for the person is available\nin a hospital or other place in the State or Territory; and\n\n(d) the person consents to the order being made;\nthe court may, without passing sentence on the person, make an\norder (in this section called a psychiatric probation order) that the\nperson reside at, or attend at, a specified hospital or other place for\nthe purpose of receiving that psychiatric treatment.\n\n(2) The court must not make an order unless the person, or the\nperson’s legal guardian, consents to the proposed treatment.\n\n(3) An order is subject to the following additional conditions:\n\n(a) that the person will, during such period, not exceeding 2\nyears, as the court specifies in the order, be subject to the\nsupervision of a probation officer appointed in accordance\nwith the order and obey all reasonable directions of a\nprobation officer so appointed;\n\n(b) that the person will be of good behaviour for such period, not\nexceeding 5 years, as the court specifies in the order.\n\n(4) The court may, on the application of the person, of the probation\nofficer appointed to supervise the person or of the person in charge\nof the hospital or other place where the treatment is being\nundertaken, vary the treatment that the person is to undertake."
    },
